\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Conditional propagators and induced vertices}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{For an [interacting selected background](fdip-005Y), put
#{n=\dim H} and #{r=\operatorname{rank}P}, and choose
orthonormal basis maps #{B:\mathbb R^r\to X} and
#{B_\perp:\mathbb R^{n-r}\to Y}. Thus a full field is
#{\phi=Bx+B_\perp y}; in this proposition #{x,y} denote coordinates.
Set
##{\begin{aligned}
A&=B^TQB,&K&=B^TQB_\perp,\\
D&=B_\perp^TQB_\perp>0,&S&=A-KD^{-1}K^T.
\end{aligned}}
The eliminated coordinates, conditionally on #{x}, have law
##{y=\mu(x)+\eta,\qquad
   \mu(x)=-D^{-1}K^Tx,\qquad \eta\sim N(0,D^{-1}).}
In particular the full conditional mean and noise covariance are
##{h(x)=Bx+B_\perp\mu(x),\qquad
   C=B_\perp D^{-1}B_\perp^T.}
The map #{h} is the harmonic extension: #{B^Th(x)=x} and
#{B_\perp^TQh(x)=0}. It is the unique minimizer of the quadratic
energy among fields with these retained coordinates.}

\p{Write #{F_\lambda(x)=F_{b,\mathcal V}(\lambda,Bx)}. Then
##{\begin{aligned}
a(x)&=\mathbb E\mathcal V(h(x)+B_\perp\eta),\\
b(x)&=\operatorname{Var}\bigl(\mathcal V(h(x)+B_\perp\eta)\bigr),\\
F_\lambda(x)&=\lambda a(x)-\frac{\lambda^2}{2}b(x)
                   +O(\lambda^3).
\end{aligned}}
Thus external mean factors in the conditional moment expansion use
#{h(x)}, and each internal pairing of eliminated coordinates uses
#{D^{-1}}. These are the background-dependent Feynman rules for this
fiber integral. In full-field components the internal pairing is
#{C}; the eliminated mean #{\mu} alone omits the retained part of
#{h}. For a coordinate selector retaining #{R} and eliminating
#{U=V\setminus R}, the formulas become
##{\mu(x)=-Q_{UU}^{-1}Q_{UR}x,\qquad
   \operatorname{Cov}(\eta)=Q_{UU}^{-1}.}}

\proof{
\p{Orthogonality preserves the volume and gives
##{\begin{aligned}
\langle\phi,Q\phi\rangle
 &=x^TAx+2x^TKy+y^TDy\\
 &=x^TSx+(y+D^{-1}K^Tx)^TD(y+D^{-1}K^Tx).
\end{aligned}}
The second term is minimized uniquely at #{y=\mu(x)}. Normalizing
its density proves the conditional law at every #{x}. Writing
#{v_x(y)=Bx+B_\perp y}, it also gives
##{\begin{aligned}
c_D&=\frac{(2\pi)^{(n-r)/2}}{\sqrt{\det D}},\\
\int_{\mathbb R^{n-r}}&e^{-\frac12\langle v_x(y),Qv_x(y)\rangle}
        e^{-\lambda\mathcal V(v_x(y))}\,dy\\
 &=c_De^{-\frac12x^TSx}e^{-F_\lambda(x)}.
\end{aligned}}
The last equality means equality after integrating each polynomial
coefficient. Hence it retains the scalar and degree-zero terms as
well as the induced polynomial vertices.}

\p{The moment series starts with
#{1-\lambda a+\lambda^2\mathbb E[\mathcal V^2\mid x]/2+O(\lambda^3)}.
Applying #{-\log(1+z)=-z+z^2/2+O(z^3)} gives the displayed
variance coefficient. In each moment, expand around #{h(x)}.
Odd centered Gaussian moments vanish; every even moment is a sum
over pairings, with one #{D^{-1}} entry per pair in eliminated
coordinates. This is
\citet{v2, Theorem 3.1 and Example 3.2, p. 29}{etingof2024mathematical}
applied to the positive form #{D}. For a quartic
#{\mathcal V(\phi)=T(\phi,\phi,\phi,\phi)/24}, the first coefficient
has degrees #{4,2,0}. In the variance, pairings with no contraction
between the two copies cancel, leaving degrees among #{6,4,2,0},
as shown in \ref{fdip-0046}. All these claims concern finite
polynomial coefficients.}
}

\p{For example, in full vertex coordinates with the symmetric
four-tensor #{T_{ijkl}} and #{h=h(x)}, Wick's three pairings give
##{\begin{aligned}
24a(x)={}&T(h,h,h,h)
 +6\sum_{i,j,k,l}T_{ijkl}h_i h_j C_{kl}\\
 &+3\sum_{i,j,k,l}T_{ijkl}C_{ij}C_{kl}.
\end{aligned}}
The covariance #{C} can be singular on #{H}: it is supported on
#{Y}, and the formula follows by applying Wick to the nonsingular
#{D^{-1}} in eliminated coordinates. No nondegeneracy on all of
#{H} is needed for this expression.}

\p{TauCeti's
[\code{integral_mul_mul_mul_multivariateGaussian}](https://github.com/TauCetiProject/TauCeti/blob/df319cee080d8cab30699ff8ab3ed8d9347768aa/TauCeti/Probability/Distributions/Gaussian/MixedMoments.lean#L162)
proves the three-pairing identity for 4 centered Gaussian
coordinates, allowing repeated indices and any positive-semidefinite
covariance. Setting that covariance to #{C} supplies the fourth-moment
step here. Contraction with #{T}, the formal logarithm and the
higher-moment pairing arguments are the additional deductions above.}

\p{The massive lattice analogue is
\citet{Theorem 8.26, p. 399}{friedli2017statistical}: on a finite
domain of #{\mathbb Z^d}, the conditional field has the massive
harmonic mean determined by its boundary values and a killed-walk
Green covariance. That theorem uses its normalized lattice Laplacian;
it does not supply the arbitrary-projector argument above. The
massless finite-domain analogue is Theorem 8.17, p. 391 of the same
source.}

\p{A formal conditional-distribution counterpart in TauCeti is
[\code{condDistrib_multivariateGaussian}](https://github.com/TauCetiProject/TauCeti/blob/df319cee080d8cab30699ff8ab3ed8d9347768aa/TauCeti/Probability/Distributions/Gaussian/Conditional.lean#L444).
It assumes a positive-semidefinite joint covariance and a
positive-definite observed covariance block, and identifies the
regular conditional law with an explicit Gaussian kernel almost
everywhere in the observed marginal. To specialize, order the
coordinates as #{Y\oplus X} and use #{G=Q^{-1}>0}. From #{QG=I},
##{\begin{aligned}
DG_{YX}+K^TG_{XX}&=0,\\
DG_{YY}+K^TG_{XY}&=I.
\end{aligned}}
Therefore #{G_{YX}G_{XX}^{-1}=-D^{-1}K^T} and
#{G_{YY}-G_{YX}G_{XX}^{-1}G_{XY}=D^{-1}}, the theorem's mean
and covariance formulas. Its almost-everywhere identification is
distinct from the explicit density kernel used here at every #{x}.
The perturbative logarithm and vertex calculations are proved above.}
